2.2. Laser metal deposition (LMD)
As Fig. 5 shows, LMD typically relies upon the feeding of powder or wire into the molten pool created by a laser beam to deposit material layer-by-layer upon a substrate. A shielding gas is used to protect the molten metal from oxidation and to carry the powder stream into the molten pool [69], [77], [85]. The process is quite like laser cladding except the need to precisely control the robot movement in three-dimensional space. The key processing parameters include laser power P, spot diameter dL, wire or powder feed rate Vf and welding speed Vs. A relatively big melt pool is necessary to hold the filler materials. The dL normally takes several millimeters. High powder diode laser provides beam spots of adjustable sizes and uniform energy distribution, and is increasingly used for LMD [86].
